Put the asynchronous guard in `AppShell.xaml.cs` so it covers toolbar, hardware, and programmatic pop navigation:

```csharp
protected override async void OnNavigating(ShellNavigatingEventArgs args)
{
    base.OnNavigating(args);

    if (!HasUnsavedChanges ||
        args.Source != ShellNavigationSource.Pop)
        return;

    var deferral = args.GetDeferral();
    try
    {
        bool discard = await CurrentPage.DisplayAlert(
            "Discard changes?",
            "Your edits have not been saved.",
            "Discard",
            "Stay");

        if (!discard)
            args.Cancel();
    }
    finally
    {
        deferral.Complete();
    }
}
```

The `finally` block completes the deferral even if the dialog throws. On `EditProductPage`, keep the normal back action but change its presentation:

```xml
<ContentPage xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2009/xaml"
             x:Class="Shop.EditProductPage">
    <Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
        <BackButtonBehavior TextOverride="Cancel"
                            IsVisible="True"
                            IsEnabled="True" />
    </Shell.BackButtonBehavior>
    <!-- form content -->
</ContentPage>
```

With no custom command, the button keeps Shell's normal back behavior while displaying `Cancel`; that pop enters `OnNavigating`, where choosing Stay cancels it.
